<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lord of Sipán</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 250 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Señor de Sipán</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lord of Sipán is the name given to a Moche ruler whose undisturbed tomb was discovered in 1987 at Huaca Rajada near Sipán on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> by the archaeologist Walter Alva. Buried around 250 CE with gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> regalia and sacrificed attendants, he is thought to have been a warrior-priest. The find transformed understanding of Moche society.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lord-of-sipan.jpg" alt="Lord of Sipán" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cicero Moraes · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Se%C3%B1or_de_S%C3%ADpan_-_Reconstrucci%C3%B3n_Facial_Forense.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The tombs lie at Huaca Rajada, near the town of Sipán in the middle of the Lambayeque Valley, in the Zaña district of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, about 20 miles east of the city of Chiclayo. Huaca Rajada is a monument of two small adobe pyramids and a low platform. The Moche built the platform and one pyramid before 300 CE; the second pyramid was added about 700 CE by a later culture. Many huacas were looted by the Spanish during and after the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a>, and looting remains a problem in many places.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Early in 1987 looters digging at Huaca Rajada found several gold objects. A quarrel among them led to the find being reported to the local police, who raided the site, recovered a number of items and alerted Walter Alva. Some archaeologists count the find among the most important in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> in the late 20th century, because the main tomb was intact and untouched by thieves.</p><p>Analysis of the skeleton shows that the Lord of Sipán was about 1.63 metres tall and some 35 to 45 years old at death. His headdresses, face mask, necklaces, nose rings, earrings and a gold pectoral with a man's head and an octopus's body show that he held the highest rank. Two necklaces of gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> shaped like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peanut">peanuts</a>, an important Moche crop, are read as symbols of the earth: gold beads on the right for the sun god and masculinity, silver on the left for the moon god and femininity.</p><p>Six people lay with him, three young women, two men who were probably warriors and a child of nine or ten, with a third man in a niche above the chamber, a dog and two llamas. The warriors' feet had been cut off, as if to keep them from leaving the tomb. The chamber held 451 ceremonial items and offerings. DNA testing and other study allowed researchers to reconstruct his face.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Later digs found a priest's tomb of the same date in 1988 and an older tomb whose occupant, related to the Lord of Sipán through the maternal line, was named the Old Lord of Sipán. By 2007 fourteen tombs had been identified. Most of the finds are in the Royal Tombs of Sipán Museum at Lambayeque, built to resemble the Moche tombs and opened in 2002 with Alva as director; a smaller museum opened at Huaca Rajada in 2009.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lord of Sipán” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Lord of Sipán</b>, buried around 250 CE in the Lambayeque valley with ornaments of gold, silver and copper, wore regalia matching that of the Warrior Priest of the Sacrifice Ceremony, and the tombs of women excavated at San José de Moro from 1991 by Christopher Donnan and…</blockquote><a href="/empire/moche">Moche · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
